# Pipeline

This file is the spine of the Sales workbook. Every raw lead that lands in
[[Data]] has to travel the same road before it can show up on the dashboard:
it gets **enriched** (filled out from the CRM), **scored** (turned into a
number we can rank on), and finally **ranked** so the best prospects float to
the top. That whole road is one unit — `:pipeline` — and it is written as a
**flow**.

A flow is orchestration, not computation. It does not do the work itself; it
*sequences* other units. The actual computing lives in [[Enrich]] and
[[Score]], which `:pipeline` only refers to by name. So this file is the place
to learn **workflows**: how `step` runs things in order, how `parallel` runs
independent work at the same time, and how the data threads through.

## Why a flow is its own thing

You could imagine inlining `enrich` and `score` right here. We do not, on
purpose. `:enrich` is a server compute unit that holds a `net` grant to reach
`api.crm.com`; `:score` is a pure server compute unit with no grants at all.
Keeping them separate means each has its own capability boundary, its own
tests, and its own contract. The flow just wires their public functions
together. That is the whole point of treating Elixir modules as contracts:
calling `Enrich.enrich/1` here *is* importing the `:enrich` unit, and the
weave gate records that as a dataflow edge in the code graph. Run
`work why :pipeline` and you will see edges into both `:enrich` and `:score`.

The shape of the road is fixed. A lead moves through these statuses, and the
flow is what advances it — the enum is just a set of atoms, the values a
`%Lead{}` can hold in its `status` field:

@statuses [:new, :enriched, :scored, :contacted]

`:pipeline` is responsible for the first three transitions — `:new ->
:enriched -> :scored`. The jump to `:contacted` belongs to the analyst agent
downstream, not here.

## The unit

The flow runs on the **server** — its placement is `server`. That matters:
`:enrich` needs the network and `:score` is cheap but we want it on the
trusted tier next to the data, so the whole orchestration lives server-side and
the browser just sees the result. Server means native Elixir on the BEAM: real
OTP, real `Task` for the parallel fan-out — not wasm.

The body reads top to bottom as the road itself. `step` names an ordered stage;
each stage takes the value the last one produced. `parallel` inside a step says
"these are independent, do them at once and join the results". The block is
self-delimiting — `flow ... do` opens it and `end` closes it; the blank lines
around it are what set it off from this prose.

flow :pipeline, placement: :server do
  @moduledoc """
  Seed -> enrich -> score -> rank. The one road every lead travels before it
  reaches the dashboard. Orchestration only: the real work is in :enrich and
  :score, referenced by name.
  """

  # Stage 1 — seed. Pull the raw leads out of the document's typed data.
  # These come in at status :new with mostly empty fields.
  step :seed do
    query(Lead) |> Enum.filter(&(&1.status == :new))
  end

  # Stage 2 — enrich, in parallel. Each lead's CRM lookup is independent of
  # every other lead's, so there is no reason to do them one at a time. We
  # fan out across the list and join. Enrich.enrich/1 fills domain / revenue /
  # employees and advances the lead to :enriched. Its :net grant to
  # api.crm.com is what makes this stage the slow one — hence the parallelism.
  step :enrich, from: :seed do
    parallel leads, max_concurrency: 8, timeout: 30_000 do
      lead -> Enrich.enrich(lead)
    end
  end

  # Stage 3 — score. Pure and fast, so we keep it sequential and simple.
  # Score.score/1 returns an integer; we stamp it onto the struct and move the
  # lead to :scored. No grants, no network — just arithmetic over fields that
  # stage 2 guaranteed are now present.
  step :score, from: :enrich do
    Enum.map(enriched, fn lead ->
      %Lead{lead | score: Score.score(lead), status: :scored}
    end)
  end

  # Stage 4 — rank. Order the scored leads best-first so the dashboard and the
  # analyst agent both consume a stable, ranked list. Ties break on revenue so
  # the bigger account wins when scores match.
  step :rank, from: :score do
    Enum.sort_by(scored, fn lead -> {lead.score, lead.revenue} end, :desc)
  end
end

## Reading the stages

A few things are worth pinning down, because they are the workflow concepts
this file exists to teach.

**`from:` is the thread.** Each `step` takes a `from:` naming the stage whose
output it consumes, and binds that output to a name (`leads`, `enriched`,
`scored`) you use in the body. There is no shared mutable state sloshing
between stages; the value produced by one stage is the *only* input to the
next. The flow is a pipeline in the literal sense — a series of pipes.

**`parallel` is for independence, not speed-for-its-own-sake.** We fan out the
enrich stage because each `Enrich.enrich/1` call is an isolated CRM round-trip
that cannot affect its neighbours. `max_concurrency: 8` caps how many of those
network calls are in flight at once so we do not hammer `api.crm.com`, and the
per-task `timeout` means one slow lookup cannot wedge the whole run. On the
server this lowers to real BEAM `Task.async_stream`; the foreign-language
units elsewhere in the workbook would lower to host-brokered concurrency
instead. Same authoring, different delivery.

**Scoring stays sequential.** [[Score]] has no grants and runs in microseconds,
so parallelising it would buy nothing and only add coordination. Reach for
`parallel` when the stages are I/O-bound and independent; leave cheap pure work
in a plain `step`.

## What comes out

Running the flow gives back a ranked list of `%Lead{}` at status `:scored`.
For the three sample accounts the workbook carries, the result is exactly the
order you would hope for — Initech first, then Acme, then Globex:

[
  %Lead{name: "Initech", domain: "initech.com", revenue: 90_000, employees: 55, score: 88, status: :scored},
  %Lead{name: "Acme",    domain: "acme.co",     revenue: 50_000, employees: 20, score: 70, status: :scored},
  %Lead{name: "Globex",  domain: "globex.io",   revenue: 12_000, employees: 8,  score: 41, status: :scored}
]

Initech leads on score and revenue, Acme sits in the middle, Globex trails —
the same ranking the [[Dashboard]] bar chart draws and the analyst agent reads
when it decides who to draft outreach for first.

## Running it

The flow is a value like any other unit, so you can run it from the document
or from a task on the [[Plan]]:

ranked = Pipeline.run()
# kick the dashboard's data region to re-render off the fresh ranking
publish(:leads, ranked)

Because `:pipeline` only calls public functions of [[Enrich]] and [[Score]],
swapping either implementation — a smarter scoring model, a different CRM
provider behind the same `:net` grant — changes nothing here. The road stays
the same; only what happens at each stop gets better.
